## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates a practical lock-free pattern for data reads that must remain consistent without heavy synchronization.

## Key Concepts
- sequence lock
- memory_order_acquire/release
- barrier considerations on x86 vs ARM

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t ver; Config cfg128; } SharedConfig;

void write_config(SharedConfig* sc, Config new_cfg) {
  __atomic_fetch_add(&sc->ver, 1, memory_order_acq_rel); // make odd
  sc->cfg128 = new_cfg;
  __atomic_fetch_add(&sc->ver, 1, memory_order_release); // make even
}

Config read_config(SharedConfig* sc) {
  Config c;
  uint64_t v;
  do {
    v = __atomic_load_n(&sc->ver, memory_order_acquire);
    if (v & 1) continue;
    c = sc->cfg128;
  } while (__atomic_load_n(&sc->ver, memory_order_acquire) != v);
  return c;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple writers?
- What are the risks on ARM with 128-bit reads?
- How would you benchmark read latency vs a simple lock?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests hands-on understanding of memory hierarchy, NUMA locality, and practical optimization trade-offs in a real kernel.

## Key Concepts

- Cache tiling for L1/L2 reuse
- NUMA first-touch locality on multi-socket systems
- Boundary handling for non-multiples of tile size
- Loop unrolling and prefetching strategies
- False-sharing avoidance via per-tile accumulation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-C kernel illustrating 32x32 tiling
void matmul_tiled(int N, const double *A, const double *B, double *C){
  for(int ii=0; ii<N; ii+=32){
    for(int jj=0; jj<N; jj+=32){
      for(int kk=0; kk<N; kk+=32){
        for(int i=ii; i< (ii+32) && i<N; ++i){
          for(int j=jj; j<(jj+32) && j<N; ++j){
            double sum = 0.0;
            for(int k=kk; k<(kk+32) && k<N; ++k){
              sum += A[i*N+k] * B[k*N+j];
            }
            C[i*N+j] += sum;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

