- How would you adapt to AVX2/AVX-512 and data layout changes to boost throughput?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about crash safety, non-volatile memory semantics, and low-level ordering. Requires explicit use of durable writes, recovery procedures, and a practical benchmark plan.

## Key Concepts

- Crash-consistency and redo-logs
- Durable writes: clwb/flushopt, sfence
- Per-slot sequencing and tail/head management
- Recovery replay and correctness guarantees

## Code Example

```pseudo
structure Slot { payload; header; seq; }
function Enqueue(buf, item): void {
  Slot s = select_next_slot();
  s.payload = item;
  s.header = make_header(++tail_seq);
  persist(s.header); // clwb/flushopt + sfence
  fence();
  tail = tail + 1; // release store
}
function Dequeue(): Item {
  if (head == tail) return EMPTY;
  Item it = read_slot(head);
  head = head + 1; // release
  persist(head);
  return it;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this across NUMA nodes?
- How would you test resilience against partial writes or power loss during enqueue?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental low-level concurrency concepts in a realistic, portable setup: a bounded, multi-producer, single-consumer ring with ABA avoidance and proper memory ordering. The problem also invites discussion of wrap-around, backpressure, and stress testing.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic fetch_add for tail and per-slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance via generation counters in slots
- Release/acquire semantics to order writes/reads
- Correct wrap-around handling and full/empty detection

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code sketch (not complete, for illustration)
struct Slot { uint64_t seq; uint8_t data[64]; };
Slot buffer[N];
_Atomic uint64_t tail = 0;
_Atomic uint64_t head = 0;

void* log_dequeue(void* out, size_t* len) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_load(&head);
  uint64_t pos = h & (N-1);
  if (buffer[pos].seq != h+1) return NULL;
  memcpy(out, buffer[pos].data, 64);
  *len = 64;
  atomic_store_explicit(&buffer[pos].seq, h+N, memory_order_release);
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, h+1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return out;
}

int log_enqueue(const void* msg, size_t len) {
  if (len > 64) return -1;
  uint64_t t = atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1);
  uint64_t pos = t & (N-1);
  while (buffer[pos].seq != t) _mm_pause();
  memcpy(buffer[pos].data, msg, 64);
  atomic_store_explicit(&buffer[pos].seq, t+1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

- Follow-up: discuss how to test for starvation, memory-order pitfalls, and cache-line alignment.

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this design for power-failure resume without data loss? 
- How to choose N and payload size for latency/throughput trade-offs in a busy IPC path?

## Why This Is Asked

A software TLB with per-core caches and cross-core invalidations tests low-level thinking about coherence, consistency, and performance under contention. It also probes trade-offs between eviction, barriers, and hardware hints.

## Key Concepts

- TLB structure: per-core caches, set-associative layout
- Page-table walk costs and caching gains
- Cross-core invalidation/shootdown mechanisms
- Memory fences and atomic updates
- Validation under memory pressure and realistic workloads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: TLB lookup and refill
function tlbLookup(vpn){
  const idx = vpn % 128;
  const e = tlb[idx];
  if (e && e.vpn === vpn && e.valid) return e.pte;
  return null;
}
function tlbRefill(vpn, pte){
  const idx = vpn % 128;
  tlb[idx] = { vpn, pte, valid: true, ts: Date.now() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to larger pages or userfaults?
- How would you validate correctness under concurrent refills and shootdowns?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests crash‑consistency and lock‑free design under PMEM constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Crash‑consistency with persistent memory using CAS, fences, and transactional updates
- Per‑thread caches with a central allocator to reduce cross‑socket traffic
- Recovery and durability: reconstruct free lists from persistent root and/or logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: pmem_alloc
function pmem_alloc() {
  if (!threadCache.empty()) return threadCache.pop();
  let batch = centralPool.acquireBatch();
  threadCache.fill(batch);
  return threadCache.pop();
}
```
